Oriel has no built-in code-signing step in the build process. Signing is a separate operation you perform after packaging. These are the manual steps to sign Windows Authenticode and self-update manifests.

# Windows Authenticode signing

Sign both the main app executable and the installer to build user trust and comply with SmartScreen reputation systems.

## Prerequisites

- A code-signing certificate (`.pfx` file with private key and certificate chain)
- The certificate password
- `osslsigncode` (for signing on Linux) or `signtool` (on Windows)

## Signing on Linux

Install `osslsigncode`:

```sh
# Arch
pacman -S osslsigncode

# Debian / Ubuntu
apt install osslsigncode
```

Sign the app executable:

```sh
osslsigncode sign -pkcs12 cert.pfx -pass "$CERT_PASS" \
  -n "My App Name" \
  -i https://example.com \
  -t http://timestamp.digicert.com \
  -in zig-out/bin/my-app.exe \
  -out zig-out/bin/my-app-signed.exe
```

Then sign the installer:

```sh
osslsigncode sign -pkcs12 cert.pfx -pass "$CERT_PASS" \
  -n "My App Name" \
  -i https://example.com \
  -t http://timestamp.digicert.com \
  -in zig-out/package/my-app-setup.exe \
  -out zig-out/package/my-app-setup-signed.exe
```

Verify the signature:

```sh
osslsigncode verify zig-out/bin/my-app-signed.exe
osslsigncode verify zig-out/package/my-app-setup-signed.exe
```

## Signing on Windows

Use `signtool` (part of the Windows SDK):

```sh
signtool sign /fd SHA256 /tr http://timestamp.digicert.com /td SHA256 /f cert.pfx /p %CERT_PASS% zig-out\bin\my-app.exe

signtool sign /fd SHA256 /tr http://timestamp.digicert.com /td SHA256 /f cert.pfx /p %CERT_PASS% zig-out\package\my-app-setup.exe
```

Verify:

```sh
signtool verify /pa zig-out\bin\my-app.exe
```

## Certificate types

- **OV (Organization Validated):** Standard code-signing certificate. SmartScreen reputation builds over time.
- **EV (Extended Validation):** Premium certificate with stricter validation. SmartScreen recognizes it immediately upon first run, avoiding reputation warnings. Requires a hardware token or cloud HSM for the private key.

For maximum compatibility, sign the executable *before* building the installer, then sign the installer separately.

# macOS code signing and notarization

`oriel package` ad-hoc signs the `.app` (`codesign --sign -`), which runs on the Mac that built it. Two better options:

## A self-signed certificate (free)

Ad-hoc signed, macOS identifies the app by the hash of that exact build: after every update the user's permission grants (Accessibility, Microphone, Screen Recording) stop applying. Signed with the same certificate every release, even a self-signed one, the app keeps its identity and the grants survive updates. Gatekeeper still asks once on first launch (**Privacy & Security → Open Anyway**).

```sh
oriel signing create --name "My App"     # a .p12 and its password in ~/.config/oriel/keys (0600); prints the SHA-1
oriel signing import ~/.config/oriel/keys/my-app-codesign.p12   # on the Mac (CI: a new unlocked keychain)
oriel package -Dmacos-sign-identity=<SHA-1>
```

Keep the certificate and reuse it for every release: a new certificate is a new identity to macOS. In CI, store the `.p12` (base64) and its password as secrets and run `oriel signing import <file> --password-env MACOS_CERT_PASSWORD` before `oriel package`.

## Developer ID and notarization

For no Gatekeeper prompt at all, sign with a Developer ID and notarize (needs an Apple developer account). `oriel package` does both:

```sh
xcrun notarytool store-credentials oriel-notary --apple-id you@example.com --team-id AB12CD34EF
oriel package -Dmacos-sign-identity="Developer ID Application: Your Name (AB12CD34EF)" \
              -Dmacos-notarize-profile=oriel-notary
```

Check the result with `spctl -a -vv App.app`.

# Self-update signing with Ed25519

Oriel's built-in updater uses Ed25519 to verify update manifests. Generate a signing key, sign your release artifacts, and embed the public key in your app.

## 1. Generate a keypair

```sh
zig build keygen -- --name myapp --out-dir ~/.config/myapp/keys
```

This creates:
- `~/.config/myapp/keys/myapp.key` (private key, mode 0600, never commit this)
- `~/.config/myapp/keys/myapp.pub` (public key, base64-encoded)

Keep the private key secure; the public key goes into your app.

## 2. Sign an update artifact

After building a release binary or AppImage:

```sh
zig build sign-update -- zig-out/bin/my-app \
  --app-id com.example.App \
  --version 1.2.0 \
  --url https://releases.example.com/my-app-1.2.0 \
  --key ~/.config/myapp/keys/myapp.key \
  --out manifest.json
```

This produces a manifest:

```json
{
  "app_id": "com.example.App",
  "version": "1.2.0",
  "target": "x86_64-linux",
  "format": "raw",
  "size": 5242880,
  "sha256": "e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855",
  "url": "https://releases.example.com/my-app-1.2.0",
  "signature": "base64-ed25519-signature"
}
```

One file for every platform (like Tauri's `latest.json`): sign each platform's artifact with its `--target`, then combine the manifests:

```sh
zig build combine-manifests -- manifest-*.json --out latest.json
```

`latest.json` has a `platforms` map (`"x86_64-linux"`, `"aarch64-macos"`, `"x86_64-windows"`, ...), each entry a complete signed manifest. Publish it at one stable URL (e.g. `https://github.com/you/app/releases/latest/download/latest.json`) and use that as `manifest_url` in every build: each app picks and verifies the entry for its own target, and an entry filed under the wrong platform is refused. A single-platform manifest still works as `manifest_url`.

## 3. Embed the public key in your app

In `build.zig`, pass the public key to `addApp`:

```zig
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    .name = "my-app",
    .root_source_file = b.path("src/main.zig"),
    .frontend = .{ .dir = "frontend" },
    .update_public_key = "base64-public-key-from-myapp.pub",
});
```

## 4. Implement updater commands

In `src/main.zig`:

```zig
const app = @import("oriel_app");

const Updater = oriel.updater.Commands(.{
    .app_id = "com.example.App",
    .manifest_url = "https://releases.example.com/latest.json",
    .current_version = "1.0.0",
    .public_key_b64 = app.update_public_key orelse @panic("missing update key"),
});

pub const Commands = struct {
    pub const updater_check = Updater.updater_check;
    pub const updater_install = Updater.updater_install;
    pub const updater_restart = Updater.updater_restart;

    pub const async_commands = .{ "updater_check", "updater_install", "updater_restart" };
};
```

## 5. Call from the frontend

```ts
import { invoke, listen } from "./oriel";

const update = await invoke("updater_check");
if (update?.available) {
    listen("updater://progress", ({ downloaded, total }) => {
        console.log(`Downloaded ${downloaded} of ${total} bytes`);
    });
    await invoke("updater_install");
    await invoke("updater_restart");
}
```

The updater downloads the new binary, verifies the Ed25519 signature, replaces the running executable atomically, and restarts the app.

# Security notes

- **Never commit private keys** to version control. Store them securely outside the repo (e.g. `~/.config/`).
- **Use HTTPS** for manifest and payload URLs in production.
- **Private keys on CI:** if you sign releases in CI, use a GitHub secret or a cloud HSM to store the private key.
- **JavaScript cannot override** the manifest URL, public key, or target path; these are baked into native code at compile time.
